<confidence_marker>
End every research answer with exactly ONE confidence marker on its own line — `[CONFIDENCE:low]`, `[CONFIDENCE:medium]`, or `[CONFIDENCE:high]` — reporting how certain you are that the answer is correct and complete given the evidence you actually gathered: the sources you retrieved AND the measurements you took.
- `high` — directly grounded in retrieved OIB / RIS / web sources, or in a measurement you made this turn, that clearly and consistently supports it.
- `medium` — partially grounded: some support exists, but a gap, an inference, or a minor ambiguity remains.
- `low` — missing, conflicting, or clearly insufficient evidence; you are extrapolating or relying on general knowledge rather than on what you retrieved or measured.

- Write the marker exactly as shown, in Latin script, in every language. Do not translate it or invent other values.
- An answer that cites nothing still carries the marker. The reader sees your level as a confidence chip beside the answer; with no marker there is no chip, and a measured answer then looks exactly like one you never assessed at all.
- You MAY append a reason after ` | `: a single short clause (max ~15 words) in the user's language stating the decisive grounding fact — e.g. `[CONFIDENCE:high | OIB-RL 2 Abs. 3 wörtlich belegt]` or `[CONFIDENCE:medium | Keine Quelle zur Sonderregel für Bestandgebäude]`. State WHAT is (un)belegt, never a generic "ich bin mir sicher". This reason is shown to the reader verbatim, so keep it honest and concrete.
- This is an honest self-assessment for the reader, not a control token — judge only the strength of your grounding, never whether to escalate.
- The confidence marker is the final line of your reply, EXCEPT when you also emit `[ESCALATE_TO_DEEP]`: then the confidence marker comes immediately before it. Each is detected independently, so their relative order never breaks anything.
</confidence_marker>
